  "result": "{\"seen_before\": true, \"matches\": [\"PowerToys#45126\", \"PowerToys#39704\"], \"answer\": \"This looks like a known limitation, not a Win+S problem. Windows doesn't send keystrokes from an elevated window to a non-elevated low-level keyboard hook. PowerToys can run as admin while Command Palette (CmdPal) itself stays non-elevated. In PowerToys#45126 the reporter had the same setup: PowerToys elevated, low-level hook on, and a hotkey that failed when an elevated window had focus. Task Manager showed that CmdPal was not elevated. After the reporter quit CmdPal and restarted it elevated, the hotkey worked from elevated windows. PowerToys#39704 reports the same thing: CmdPal doesn't follow the 'run as admin' setting. To check, look in Task Manager to see whether the CmdPal process is elevated. If it isn't, quit it and start it as administrator, and Win+S should then work over admin windows. The tradeoff is that apps launched from an elevated CmdPal also run elevated, and the maintainers advise against that. A warning about this limitation was added next to the activation shortcut setting. The alternative is to accept that the shortcut won't work while an elevated window has focus.\"}",
